The use of antibiotics in the treatment of parasitic infections can lead to the development and spread of antibiotic-resistant strains through the following ways:

1. Selection of resistant bacteria: When antibiotics are used in the treatment of pathogenic infections, they can cause the development of antibiotic-resistant strains of bacteria. This is because the bacteria that are not resistant to the antibiotic may be killed, while the bacteria that are resistant to the antibiotic may become more common.

2. Mutation and mutation transfer: Bacteria can mutate and develop resistance to antibiotics. This can occur through the presence of specific genes that allow the bacteria to survive in the presence of the antibiotic. These genes can be transferred between bacteria, allowing the spread of antibiotic resistance among the population.

3. Competition with beneficial bacteria: The use of antibiotics can also lead to the development of antibiotic-resistant strains by causing the development of competition with beneficial bacteria. This can lead to the development of antibiotic-resistant strains, as the beneficial bacteria are more likely to be killed by the antibiotic, while the resistant strains are more likely to outcompete the beneficial bacteria and become more common.

