A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum height is reached when the velocity of the particle becomes zero. Using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2as \]  
where:  
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (final velocity at maximum height)  
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \) (initial velocity)  
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)  
- \( s = \text{height} \) (the maximum height)  

Substituting the values:  
\[ 0 = (20)^2 + 2(-9.8)s \]  
\[ 0 = 400 - 19.6s \]  
\[ 19.6s = 400 \]  
\[ s = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.20 \, \text{m} \]  

Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is \(\boxed{20.20 \, \text{m}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
