\text{Upper Limit (upper)} = \frac{1}{1 - \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{L_1} - \frac{1}{L_2} \right)}
\]  
\[
\text{Lower Limit (lower)} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{L_1} - \frac{1}{L_2} \right)}
\]  
where \( L_1 \) and \( L_2 \) are the lower and upper limits of the two components.  

This is the **Crourou-Chapman equation** (or **Condon-Chapstone equation**).  

**Explanation:**  
- The equation assumes ideal behavior of the mixture.  
- It provides a weighted average of the limits of the individual components.  
- The equation is valid for mixtures of two gases.  
- The results are most accurate when the two components have similar flammability limits.  

For mixtures with more than two components, the equation is not applicable.  

\[
\boxed{\text{Condon-Chapstroff equation}}
