","To find the frequency domain representation of the function \( f(t) = 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \), we use the Fourier transform formula:
\[
F(f) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
Substituting \( f(t) \), we get:
\[
F(f) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} (3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t)) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
This can be separated into two integrals:
\[
F(f) = \frac{3}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt + \frac{2}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]
\[
F(f) = \frac{3}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt + \frac{1}{\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-i2\pi ft} \, dt
\]

