","To find the length of the train, we use the formula:  
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
\]  
Given:  
- Joger's speed = 9 km/hr  
- Train's speed = 45 km/hr  
- Distance between the joger and the gate = 150 m  
- Time taken for the train to pass the joger = 25 s  

First, convert the speeds to meters per second (m/s):  
\[
\text{Joger's speed} = 9 \text{ km/hr} = 9 \times \frac{1000}{3600} = 25.5 \text{ m/s}
\]  
\[
\text{Train's speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000}{3600} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]  
The train's speed relative to the joger is the difference in their speeds:  
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 25.5 \text{ m/s} = -13.5 \text{ m/s}
\]  
The negative sign indicates that the train is moving towards the jugger. The distance traveled by the train to pass the juguer is the sum of the initial distance between them and the length of the train.  

The time taken for the train to pass the jugrer is 25 s. During this time, the train moves 150 m (the initial distance) and the length of the train.  

Relative speed = \( \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}} \).  
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time}
\]  
\[
\text{Distance} = 13.5 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 337.5 \text{ m}
\]  
Thus, the total distance traveled by the train is 337.5 m.  
\[
\text{Total distance} = 150 \text{ m} + \text{Length of the train}
\]  
\[
337.5 \text{ m} = 150 \text{ m} + \text{Length of the train}
\]  
\[
\text{Length of the train} = 337.5 \text{ m} - 150 \text{ m} = 187.5 \text{ m}
\]  

Thus, the length of the train is \(\boxed{187.5 \text{ m}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
