At time \( t \), \( b \) starts 3 hours after \( a \). Thus, \( a \) travels \( t + 3 \) hours.
The speed of \( a \) is 10 kmph, so the distance traveled by \( a \) is:
\[
a = 10(t + 3) = 10t + 30
\]
The speed of \( b \) is 20 kmph, so the distance traveled by \( b \) is:
\[
b = 20t
\]
When \( b \) catches up with \( a \), they will be at the same distance from the starting point. Thus, \( a = b \).
\[
10t + 30 = 20t
\]
Subtract \( 10t \) from both sides:
\[
30 = 10t
\]
Divide both sides by 10:
\[
t = 3 \text{ hours}
\]
Now, find the distance traveled by \( b \):
\[
b = 20t = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
The distance traveled by \( a \) is:
\[
a = 10(t + 3) = 10(3 + 3) = 10 \times 6 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
