\Delta \lambda = \lambda_0 \left( \frac{1 - \cos \theta}{2} \right)
\]  
For maximum shift (\( \theta = 180^\circ \)), \( \cos 180^\circ = -1 \), so:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_0 \left( \frac{1 - (-1)}{2} \right) = \lambda_0 \left( \frac{2}{2} \right) = \lambda_0
\]  
The percentage change in wavelength is:  
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} \times 100\% = \frac{\lambda_0}{\lambda_0} \times 100\% = 100\%
\]  
Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{100\%}\).
