A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The electric force acting on the proton is given by:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
Using Newton's second law, the acceleration of the proton is:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) Using the kinematic equation for acceleration:  
\[
v = v_0 + at,
\]  
where \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) (since the proton starts from rest), and \(a = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\), we solve for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, it takes \(\boxed{7.25 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\) for the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).
