\[
F = \frac{Gm^2}{r^2}
\]

where:
- \( F = 3600 \, \text{N} \) (gravitational force)
- \( G = 8.87 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{N m}^2/\text{kg}^2 \) (gravitational constant)
- \( m = 1000 \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the Earth)
- \( r = 10^5 \, \text{m} \) (radius of the Earth)

Substituting the values:

\[
3600 = \frac{8.87 \times 10^{-5} \times m^2}{r^2}
\]

Solving for \( m \):

\[
m = \sqrt{\frac{3600 \times r^2}{8.87 \times 10^{-5}}}
\]

Substituting \( r = 10^5 \, \text{m} \):

\[
m = \sqrt{\frac{3600 \times (10^5)^2}{8.87 \times 10^{-5}}}
\]

\[
m = \sqrt{\frac{3600 \times 10^6}{8.87 \times 10^{-5}}}
\]

\[
m = \sqrt{4.05 \times 10^{12}}
\]

\[
m = 2.01 \times 10^6 \, \text{kg}
\]

The kinetic energy (KE) of the satellite is given by:

\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
\]

where:
- \( m = 2.01 \times 10^6 \, \text{kg} \) (mass of the satellite)
- \( v = 800 \, \text{m/s} \) (velocity of the satellite)

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{KE} = \frac{1}{2} \times 2.01 \times 10^6 \times (800)^2
\]

\[
\text{KE} = 0.5 \times 2.01 \times 10^6 \times 640000
\]

\[
\text{KE} = 1.206 \times 10^{12} \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the kinetic energy of the satellite is \(\boxed{1.21 \times 10^{12} \, \text{J}}\).
