Here, \( \Delta E \) is the energy uncertainty and \( \Delta t \) is the lifetime. For a particle with a well-defined mass \( E_0 \), the uncertainty in energy is related to the lifetime by:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{2\pi \Delta t}
\]  
Substituting \( \Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \) s and \( \hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \) J·s (or \( 1.054 \times 10^{-32} \) J·s), the uncertainty in energy is:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{2\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23}} \approx 8.4 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV}
\]  
Converting to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E \approx 8.4 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{10^6 \text{ eV}} \approx 8.4 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the uncertainty in the energy is \( 8.4 \times 10^{-18} \) MeV.  

The range of outcomes of measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is:  
\[
E_0 \pm \Delta E = 1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 8.4 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}
\]  
The uncertainty is negligible compared to the energy, so the range is:  
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 8.4 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}
\]  
However, this is a purely theoretical range based on the uncertainty principle. In practice, experimental measurements have uncertainties on the order of \( 10^{-3} \) MeV or smaller.  

